litedoc4 is Apache-2.0, and most of its dependency closure is dual-licensed
"MIT OR Apache-2.0": taking the Apache-2.0 option puts those crates under terms
LICENSE and this file already carry, which is why they are not listed here. The
crates below offer no such choice, so their own notices are reproduced.

This list covers the Rust dependency closure, and it is here because a compiled
`litedoc4` used to be published as a release archive: an archive is Object form
and carries that closure inside it. **Nothing is distributed in Object form any
more** — a consumer requires this package and Lake builds it from source — so the
half of `tools/provenance-gate.sh` that rebuilt this section from `cargo tree`
went with the release workflow it existed for (`docs/provenance.md` §7-8). While
`crates/` is in the tree this section is a reviewed list like the rest of the
file. If a binary is ever published again, the derived check comes back with it.

It covers the whole normal dependency closure, including crates that only run at
compile time. A proc-macro's own code is not linked into the binary, but the code
it generates is derived from it, and a notice printed for one crate too many
costs a paragraph while a missing one is an unmet obligation.

